    genres: ['History', 'Biography', 'Aviation'],
    rating: 4.5,
    review: 'What an amazing story of two pioneers of the modern world. Of course I\'d heard of the Wright brothers, but I had no idea of the depth of the story. Reading this feels like a guidebook to the scientific method. The ignorance of the media is hilariously absurd, reminds me of Walt Disney\'s lesson that distributors/networks/media don\'t care until the public cares. But the Wrights just got on with it, not a care, they were builders and experimenters not fame seekers. The book ending is rather bleak though. Years wasted fighting patents, even knowing they won, feels like it must have been torture. The Smithsonian attempting to discredit them and promote Langley instead was disgusting and reminded me of a similar story with George Stephenson (the father of railways) when he created a miners\' safety lamp and the Royal Society accused him of stealing it from a celebrated scientist because he was “only” an engineer. Of course we know it worked out in the end, but it doesn\'t make it any less awful to know these legends (or more so Orville as his brother had earlier died) were put through this. Still, an incredible story and exciting read that I flew through in a couple days.',
    description: 'In this fascinating, highly readable biography, Fred C. Kelly, a former newspaperman, author, and an old friend of the Wrights, tells the story of the two brilliant, dedicated, flight-obsessed bicycle mechanics from Ohio who first realized mankind\'s age-old dream of conquering the skies. Long considered the definitive Wright biography (the manuscript was read and approved by Orville Wright), this book recounts the Wrights\' small-town boyhood, their early interest in all things mechanical, the establishment of the Wright Cycle Shop, and the complete behind-the-scenes story of how they designed, built, tested, and flew (December 1903) the first "Flyer."\n\nEnhanced with sixteen rare photographs, Mr. Kelly\'s engaging account avoids minute technical description, yet describes simply and clearly the technological innovations that enabled the two brothers to succeed where so many others had failed. Anyone interested in the mechanics of flight or early aviation will find this volume a splendid introduction to the Wright brothers and their epochal achievement.',
